{
  "sections": {
    "cortisol": {
      "type": "custom-advertorial-kapoor",
      "blocks": {
        "lede_open": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Cortisol gets written about as though it were a toxin. It isn’t. It is the hormone that gets you through a hard morning - mobilising glucose, sharpening attention, holding non-urgent maintenance until later. Every one of those is useful. All of them are borrowed against later.</p><p>A stressful year is a year of borrowing without a repayment window. Here is where the bill lands, in the order most people notice it.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
        "head_list": {
          "type": "section_head",
          "settings": {
            "label": "The list",
            "title": "What chronic stress actually costs, in order.",
            "align": "center",
            "small": false,
            "tight": false
          }
        },
        "rank01": {
          "type": "rank",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "01",
            "label": "Sleep architecture",
            "title": "The night stops being restorative",
            "text": "<p>Cortisol is meant to bottom out overnight and rise before you wake. Under sustained stress that curve flattens: you fall asleep fine, then surface at three, and the deep sleep where most repair is scheduled gets cut short.</p>",
            "aside_label": "Where you see it",
            "aside_text": "Sleep duration unchanged, sleep quality and deep sleep down. The first thing people notice.",
            "dark": true
          }
        },
        "rank02": {
          "type": "rank",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "02",
            "label": "Autonomic tone",
            "title": "HRV drifts down and stays there",
            "text": "<p>Heart rate variability is a readout of how relaxed your nervous system is overnight. A braced system produces narrow, metronomic beats. This is the metric that flattens while your training is unchanged, which is why it is so often blamed on the programme.</p>",
            "aside_label": "Where you see it",
            "aside_text": "A weekly HRV average that stopped climbing. Read the trend, not one morning.",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "rank03": {
          "type": "rank",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "03",
            "label": "Fuel handling",
            "title": "Energy arrives in spikes, not a line",
            "text": "<p>One of cortisol’s jobs is to raise blood glucose for a demand that is about to arrive. When the demand is a spreadsheet rather than a sprint, that glucose has nowhere to go. The pattern people describe is a wired morning and a flat afternoon.</p>",
            "aside_label": "Where you see it",
            "aside_text": "The three o’clock dip, and the coffee that replaced doing anything about it.",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "rank04": {
          "type": "rank",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "04",
            "label": "Deferred maintenance",
            "title": "Repair keeps getting pushed to tomorrow",
            "text": "<p>Under threat the body prioritises immediate survival over long-term upkeep. Tissue repair, immune housekeeping and structural maintenance are all deferrable, and a stress response that never switches off keeps deferring them. That is the queue that never clears.</p>",
            "aside_label": "Where you see it",
            "aside_text": "Niggles that take longer to settle. Every cold in the office arriving at your desk.",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "rank05": {
          "type": "rank",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "05",
            "label": "Cellular energy",
            "title": "The budget the other four are drawn against",
            "text": "<p>Everything above is an energy transaction. Mitochondria fund it using NAD⁺, and repair enzymes need the same coenzyme to work at all. Sustained stress raises the demand while age steadily lowers the supply - and when supply is short, energy is paid first and repair takes what is left.</p>",
            "aside_label": "Where you see it",
            "aside_text": "Nowhere directly. No wearable reports on it, which is exactly why it is the one nobody optimises.",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "statement_budget": {
          "type": "statement",
          "settings": {
            "text": "Stress raises the repair bill. It does nothing about the budget."
          }
        },
        "head_correction": {
          "type": "section_head",
          "settings": {
            "label": "The correction",
            "title": "You can’t supplement your way out of a stressful year.",
            "align": "center",
            "small": false,
            "tight": false
          }
        },
        "lede_correction": {
          "type": "lede",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Nothing in a capsule fixes a workload, a bad manager or a small child who wakes at five. The things that lower cortisol are structural: sleep, daylight, training that is hard sometimes rather than hard always, and time not spent braced. Those come first, and no supplement replaces them.</p><p>What is addressable is the other side of the ledger. If stress raises the repair bill, the useful question is whether your cells have the energy to pay it - and unlike your calendar, that part is an input you can change.</p>",
            "align": "left"
          }
        },
        "correction1": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "2",
            "label": "Not this",
            "title": "Lowering the number",
            "text": "<p>MUTO is not a cortisol blocker and does not claim to be. Cortisol is a signal, and suppressing a signal is not the same as resolving what caused it.</p>",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "correction2": {
          "type": "card_row",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "",
            "columns": "2",
            "label": "This",
            "title": "Funding the recovery",
            "text": "<p>Supporting the cellular energy and repair capacity that a demanding period draws down hardest.</p>",
            "dark": true
          }
        },
