        "faq1": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Does MUTO lower cortisol?",
            "answer": "<p>No, and it does not claim to. Cortisol is a signal, and lowering a signal is not the same as resolving what produced it. The things that genuinely lower it are structural - sleep, daylight, training load, and time not spent braced. MUTO works on the other side of the ledger: the cellular energy and repair capacity that a demanding period draws down hardest.</p>",
            "open_by_default": true
          }
        },
        "faq2": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is this an adaptogen?",
            "answer": "<p>No. Adaptogens such as ashwagandha or rhodiola act on the stress response itself. MUTO is not one and does not contain them. It works a layer beneath, on NAD⁺ - the coenzyme that funds cellular energy production and repair.</p>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "faq3": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "I’m stressed and exhausted. Will I feel it quickly?",
            "answer": "<p>There are no stimulants in it, so nothing in the first hour. Most people report the afternoon dip going in weeks three to four, and wearable data typically moves between day 30 and day 60. If exhaustion is severe or persistent, see a doctor first - it deserves a diagnosis rather than a supplement.</p>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "faq4": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What if my data doesn’t move?",
            "answer": "<p>Then you send it back. The 90-day money-back guarantee exists because the honest test is your own numbers, not how the marketing reads. Log your 30-day HRV and resting heart rate average before you start so the comparison means something.</p>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        }
